    "question": {
      ":question/id": "20bc4d29-134e-541a-9a01-48d750a32016",
      ":question/problem": "Given $y'(t)=2+t^{3}$, find $y(t)$, the antiderivative of $y'(t)$.\n\n$y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $y'(t)=2+t^{3}$.\n\nApplying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(2+t^{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t &= \\int \\left(2+t^{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= 2\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\int t^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= 2\\frac{t^{0+1}}{(0+1)}+\\frac{t^{3+1}}{(3+1)}+C \\\\ &= 2t+\\frac{t^{4}}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
